 infinite Taylor series equals $f$ near $a$.","$f(x)-P_k(x)=o((x-a)^k)$ as $x\\to a$.","$f(x)-P_k(x)=O((x-a)^{k+1})$ as $x\\to a$.","$f(x)=P_k(x)$ throughout some neighbourhood of $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c7b4b49704b180d36a492c4","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $f(1) = 2$, $f'(1) = 3$, and $f''(1) = 4$. What is the second-order Taylor expansion polynomial part at $a = 1$?","options":["$2+4(x-1)+3(x-1)^2$","$2+3(x-1)+2(x-1)^2$","$2+3x+4x^2$","$2+3(x-1)+4(x-1)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"36fb0ec9ad4354f240e562f4","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\dfrac{1}{1-x} = 1+x+x^2+h_2(x)x^2$, what must be true of $h_2(x)$ as $x\\to 0$?","options":["$h_2(x)\\to 1$","$h_2(x)\\to\\infty$","$h_2(x)\\to -1$","$h_2(x)\\to 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"feeefce55ad56ca35a0a0664","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_013","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f$ be twice differentiable at $0$ with $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{f(x)}{x^3}=1$. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{f'(x)}{x^2}=3$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{f''(x)}{x}=6$","$f''(0)=0$","$f'''(0)=6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"27548f8c959c568b0158c8f1","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$. Which statement about the hypotheses of its second-order Peano Taylor formula at $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$ is correct?","options":["Equality with an infinite Taylor series near $a$ is required.","Twice differentiability at $a$ suffices; real analyti